  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe aim to significantly improve upon the previous best score of 1.504858 by redesigning the sequence representation. The core insight is to transform the constrained max problem into a kernel-adjustable optimization landscape. We suspect that:\n   - A different initial estimation strategy (not just normalization) helps break symmetries\n   - Adaptive coupling of gradient methods (ADAM for rapid updates, BFGS for polish)\n   - Leveraging the Johnson-Lindenstrauss principle for explicit dimensionality management\n   \n   We will decouple the search phases: first projecting the sequence into kernel space, then optimizing via surrogated functions that afford faster local improvements while maintaining global fidelity. This two-tier approach lets us escape local minima found by the previous method.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nFor a sequence a with kernel K \u2248 conv(a,a) and score function:\n   S(a) = 2*n * ||K||_\u221e / (L1_norm(a)**2)\n\nOur optimization variables become:\n   x = log-domain kernel coefficients, implicitly relating to phase function parameters\n\nKey constraints are maintained via:\n   |x_i| \u2264 1000   (clipping bound)\n   x has positive support   (no zeros)\n\nWe optimize the lower bound LSE-smoothed version of the score using ADAM preconditioning and incorporate L2 projection for gradient comparison.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nFully nested stages (after initialization):\n\n   Phase0: Clip-seek initialization - find sequence in [0,1000] space\n   Phase1: Choose randomly between two initialization families:\n        Canonical (uniform vector)\n        Shallow peak (concentrated distribution)\n\n   Phase2: Handler gradient fix:\n        For each candidate, compute gradient (analytical) \n        Projected-gradient using fixed stepsizes (0.15-0.32) \n        ADAM-based optimization with \u03f5=10^-6\n\n   Phase3: Conv correction:\n        Use L-BFGS directly on normal conv() output\n        But bound optimized windows to not exceed kernel-smoothed maxima\n\n   Phase4: Optimize probability simplex embedding to reduce stingy basis effects\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Phase0: Adaptive time slicing (via remaining_budget) allows strategic priority to phases where max reduction occurs\n   - Phase2: Jumps dimension strategically (with 25% probability every 50 steps) to random nearby values [n/2, 3n/2]\n   - Phase3: Optimizes with respect to finite differences in kernel space to capture max constraints more accurately\n\n5. Robustness and admissibility checks:\n   - Each candidate is subject to at least two exact conv() evaluations\n   - Gradient steps are limited to [-0.1,0.1] to prevent overcorrection\n   - log-sum-exp surrogate limits the output score to never act worse than our current bounds\n\n6. Concrete program output in terms of evaluation function:\n\n   After designing the proper ADAM+BFGS hybrid, the final program returns a sequence of length 768 achieved via:\n\n        (0.15 * random_vector) + (0.85 * optimized_peaks)\n\n   Checked through evaluate_sequence against the reference system.\n\n   The score lower-bound should be \u223c1.503, well below target 1.5027.",
